WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.903
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4521.

2
00:00:00.903 --> 00:00:01.806
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:01.806 --> 00:00:02.108
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alrighty.

4
00:00:02.108 --> 00:00:03.613
Today is September twenty-fourth, 2015.

5
00:00:03.613 --> 00:00:09.032
I'm Chelsea Arseneault representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

6
00:00:09.032 --> 00:00:13.849
We're working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

7
00:00:13.849 --> 00:00:17.161
Today we're at the Rigmaiden Recreation Center on Old Spanish Trail.

8
00:00:17.161 --> 00:00:19.570
I'm here today again with Mr. Barry Edwards.

9
00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.581
Thank you so much for agreeing to be interviewed again.

10
00:00:22.581 --> 00:00:24.387
This is our second interview together.

11
00:00:24.387 --> 00:00:27.398
Our first one was on Oral History Day in March.

12
00:00:27.398 --> 00:00:28.000
<v BARRY EDWARDS>Right

13
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:37.123
<v ARSENEAULT>So I listened to that interview, and I have some like clarification questions that I wanted to ask.

14
00:00:37.123 --> 00:00:43.205
You said your mother was a . . . Your mother's maiden name was Prater?

15
00:00:43.205 --> 00:00:43.712
<v EDWARDS>Right.

16
00:00:43.712 --> 00:00:44.219
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

17
00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:47.767
So who was your mom related to?

18
00:00:47.767 --> 00:00:50.808
Like that side of the family?

19
00:00:50.808 --> 00:00:54.356
<v EDWARDS>On the Prater side of the family?

20
00:00:54.356 --> 00:00:55.370
The Braxton’s.

21
00:00:55.370 --> 00:00:56.384
The Rigmaiden’s.

22
00:00:56.384 --> 00:00:56.890
LaDoux’s.

23
00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.918
I think that's it.

24
00:00:58.918 --> 00:00:59.425
Yeah.

25
00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Does she ever talk about that side of the family?

26
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:05.990
<v EDWARDS>Not really.

27
00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.959
She never really talked about them.

28
00:01:08.959 --> 00:01:12.918
Matter of fact, since you brought that up,

29
00:01:12.918 --> 00:01:23.311
about three years ago I went to a family reunion of her side of the family, the Rigmaiden’s and the LaDoux’s.

30
00:01:23.311 --> 00:01:25.786
This was in Beaumont [Texas].

31
00:01:25.786 --> 00:01:35.189
And I got the chance to meet a lot of my cousins that I’d been knowing for a while,

32
00:01:35.189 --> 00:01:40.138
but I didn't know we were cousins about the relationship.

33
00:01:40.138 --> 00:01:46.571
That's how we . . . You know my mom didn't talk too much about it.

34
00:01:46.571 --> 00:01:48.551
The kinship and all.

35
00:01:48.551 --> 00:01:53.005
But we would always spend a little time together.

36
00:01:53.005 --> 00:01:56.469
Certain . . . With certain parts of the family.

37
00:01:56.469 --> 00:02:01.418
Especially the Braxton’s, because that was her mom's last name.

38
00:02:01.418 --> 00:02:04.388
Yeah, her mom was [Middy?] Braxton.

39
00:02:04.388 --> 00:02:07.357
And her dad was a Prater.

40
00:02:07.357 --> 00:02:10.821
So that's how that part got together.

41
00:02:10.821 --> 00:02:15.276
But like the Braxton’s we grew up around them.

42
00:02:15.276 --> 00:02:22.699
Friends, played ball, all sports together and all so we knew that part of it.

43
00:02:22.699 --> 00:02:30.122
But any and other part like the Rigmaiden’s or whatever, we didn't get that deep.

44
00:02:30.122 --> 00:02:37.051
We just . . . pretty much like first cousins like yeah . . . That's the way that worked.

45
00:02:37.051 --> 00:02:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the Braxton's side of the family like?

46
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:42.457
<v EDWARDS>Wow.

47
00:02:42.457 --> 00:02:50.677
A lot of fun. [laughs] Yes, as far as I can remember we had a real good time.

48
00:02:50.677 --> 00:02:59.811
Now back in the day, now this was before I was born, on the Braxton side there was a minister.

49
00:02:59.811 --> 00:03:07.118
He was a Braxton at the church where we belonged right now, at the Mount Zion

50
00:03:07.118 --> 00:03:08.031
<c.p>Baptist Church.

51
00:03:08.031 --> 00:03:14.425
So if you go through that history you'll find Braxton’s you know as part . . .

52
00:03:14.425 --> 00:03:15.339
<v ARSENEAULT>Reverend Griffin?

53
00:03:15.339 --> 00:03:17.165
Was it Griffin Braxton?

54
00:03:17.165 --> 00:03:18.535
<v EDWARDS>It may been.

55
00:03:18.535 --> 00:03:22.646
Like I said I didn't . . . I didn't know him.

56
00:03:22.646 --> 00:03:29.039
And I guess I was just one of them little wild kids you know.

57
00:03:29.039 --> 00:03:31.780
I like to have fun, whatever.

58
00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:34.976
But I didn't get to know them.

59
00:03:34.976 --> 00:03:39.087
Like I said, he was before I was born.

60
00:03:39.087 --> 00:03:45.480
But I never really got into like family history and all like this so.

61
00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:50.047
Matter of fact Kim's mom, been knowing her for years.

62
00:03:50.047 --> 00:03:59.181
Butch Lemelle her brother . . . Today was the first day that I put it together that they were brother and sister.

63
00:03:59.181 --> 00:04:03.748
And I just brought it up to them just now.

64
00:04:03.748 --> 00:04:06.945
After all these years of knowing them.

65
00:04:06.945 --> 00:04:10.142
I been knowing them all my life,

66
00:04:10.142 --> 00:04:17.906
but to know that they're brother and sister that's new to me . . . Finally putting it all together.

67
00:04:17.906 --> 00:04:32.063
So on that part of it I was . . . Other than having fun with sports and swimming and all that there's . . . We never really talked about family trees and all like this.

68
00:04:32.063 --> 00:04:36.173
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were some of the Braxton’s you played with?

69
00:04:36.173 --> 00:04:38.000
I'm just curious.

70
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:45.139
<v EDWARDS>[Darnell?] he was around my age and he passed away right after high school.

71
00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:53.808
He was closest to my age, and they had James which is one of his older brothers.

72
00:04:53.808 --> 00:04:59.417
He would deal with my older brothers like Kenney and Sunny.

73
00:04:59.417 --> 00:05:04.007
Well McKeever Junior . . . But it was five of us.

74
00:05:04.007 --> 00:05:10.126
Darnell . . . he was the only one that was pretty . . . Around my age.

75
00:05:10.126 --> 00:05:21.854
His sisters and all were older and younger, so I never got a chance to know too much about the sisters and all.

76
00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:25.934
But they were a pretty nice sized family.

77
00:05:25.934 --> 00:05:31.033
We was about that same in number in the family.

78
00:05:31.033 --> 00:05:39.702
They might have had maybe . . . Maybe two or three more siblings than what we had in ours.

79
00:05:39.702 --> 00:05:40.722
It was

80
00:05:40.722 --> 00:05:49.391
<c.p>eight of us, and if I'm not mistaken they had like eleven or twelve in their family.

81
00:05:49.391 --> 00:05:53.470
<v ARSENEAULT>And what are all of your sibling’s names?

82
00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:55.000
In order?

83
00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:03.470
<v EDWARDS>In order my siblings: Ester, Brenda . . . Brenda passed away in ninety-two.

84
00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:09.630
McKeever Junior, Kenny . . . Everybody called him Kenny boy.

85
00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:11.170
And myself.

86
00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:14.250
Cathy, Miranda, and Melissa.

87
00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:18.100
It was eight of us.

88
00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:18.870
Yeah.

89
00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:22.720
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all all play sports?

90
00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:25.030
<v EDWARDS>The boys, yes.

91
00:06:25.030 --> 00:06:34.270
Cathy, the one next to me, younger than me, she played tennis.

92
00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:35.040
Yeah.

93
00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:40.430
So pretty much yeah that's about it.

94
00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:47.360
About four of us total that played the sports.

95
00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:55.060
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of . . . What kind of things did y'all play?

96
00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:56.600
<v EDWARDS>Mostly football.

97
00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.680
Football, baseball, ran track.

98
00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:02.760
Myself and McKeever Junior.

99
00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.840
We both ran track.

100
00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:10.460
Basketball . . . And that's pretty much it.

101
00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:12.000
Yeah.

102
00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:14.609
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you win any awards?

103
00:07:14.609 --> 00:07:17.217
<v EDWARDS>I don't think I did.

104
00:07:17.217 --> 00:07:20.870
It's kind of far back right now.

105
00:07:20.870 --> 00:07:25.565
Scoring titles . . . I think I won one of those.

106
00:07:25.565 --> 00:07:31.304
I was in competition with a young man around that time.

107
00:07:31.304 --> 00:07:34.957
He was a LaDoux from Sulphur [Louisiana].

108
00:07:34.957 --> 00:07:39.130
Of course we were in a different category.

109
00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:41.739
He would play for Sulphur

110
00:07:41.739 --> 00:07:46.435
<c.p>High and I would play for single A School.

111
00:07:46.435 --> 00:07:58.435
But he and I were . . . I guess you could say in competition during the years as far as who’s running the most touchdowns,

112
00:07:58.435 --> 00:08:07.826
or they give it to us every week as how many touchdowns we had collectively and all this.

113
00:08:07.826 --> 00:08:13.043
And that was all that comes to mind right there.

114
00:08:13.043 --> 00:08:21.391
But as far as Heisman trophies or anything like that, no. No awards; nothing like that.

115
00:08:21.391 --> 00:08:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What about your brothers?

116
00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:25.966
<v EDWARDS>Pretty much the same.

117
00:08:25.966 --> 00:08:33.831
Nothing I can think of as . . . that really stands out none other than individual little accomplishments.

118
00:08:33.831 --> 00:08:37.271
Nothing to broadcast or anything like that.

119
00:08:37.271 --> 00:08:43.169
<v ARSENEAULT>I think I saw an article in the paper from like 1964.

120
00:08:43.169 --> 00:08:49.559
It was for McKeever Edwards getting some kind of scoring something or other?

121
00:08:49.559 --> 00:08:50.051
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

122
00:08:50.051 --> 00:08:55.458
<v ARSENEAULT>I didn't know what it . . . I was like maybe he's related?

123
00:08:55.458 --> 00:08:56.932
I'm not sure.

124
00:08:56.932 --> 00:08:58.898
<v EDWARDS>Yeah that's my brother.

125
00:08:58.898 --> 00:09:06.271
Matter of fact I was trying to think . . . He graduated from high school in sixty-five.

126
00:09:06.271 --> 00:09:10.203
So it must have been his junior year.

127
00:09:10.203 --> 00:09:15.610
They must have did a state championship or something like that.

128
00:09:15.610 --> 00:09:16.102
Yeah.

129
00:09:16.102 --> 00:09:21.017
That's . . . We called him Sunny but his name McKeever Junior.

130
00:09:21.017 --> 00:09:22.000
Yeah.

131
00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.026
<v ARSENEAULT>I wanted to ask just . . .

132
00:09:23.026 --> 00:09:23.231
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

133
00:09:23.231 --> 00:09:27.128
<v ARSENEAULT>I just to make sure I was like this is . . . I know you said your dad's name was McKeever.

134
00:09:27.128 --> 00:09:27.333
<v EDWARDS>Right.

135
00:09:27.333 --> 00:09:30.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I was like it can't be his dad, because it was 1964.

136
00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:30.431
<v EDWARDS>Right.

137
00:09:30.431 --> 00:09:35.169
<v ARSENEAULT>So it must be either a cousin or brother or something.

138
00:09:35.169 --> 00:09:35.600
<v EDWARDS>Right.

139
00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:38.185
Now don't get me wrong, now.

140
00:09:38.185 --> 00:09:42.923
My dad finished night school in ‘65 if I'm not mistaken.

141
00:09:42.923 --> 00:09:48.523
He and my oldest brother McKeever Junior graduated high school the same year.

142
00:09:48.523 --> 00:09:48.954
Yeah.

143
00:09:48.954 --> 00:09:54.554
He graduated from night school, and of course my brother graduated from school.

144
00:09:54.554 --> 00:09:58.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was he going to night school?

145
00:09:58.000 --> 00:09:58.571
<v EDWARDS>At Mossville.

146
00:09:58.571 --> 00:09:58.857
Here.

147
00:09:58.857 --> 00:09:59.143
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

148
00:09:59.143 --> 00:09:59.429
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

149
00:09:59.429 --> 00:10:00.857
They had a set up.

150
00:10:00.857 --> 00:10:02.571
He went to night school here.

151
00:10:02.571 --> 00:10:03.143
They graduated.

152
00:10:03.143 --> 00:10:05.429
I think they marched down the aisle together.

153
00:10:05.429 --> 00:10:07.429
<v ARSENEAULT>That'd be a great picture to get.

154
00:10:07.429 --> 00:10:08.000
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

155
00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:10.712
<v ARSENEAULT>Get a picture of that scanned in.

156
00:10:10.712 --> 00:10:19.238
<v EDWARDS>Yes, if I . . . Don't get me wrong we may have one but I would have to . . . We would have to dig through . . .

157
00:10:19.238 --> 00:10:19.625
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

158
00:10:19.625 --> 00:10:22.725
<v EDWARDS>All the stuff we have at the house.

159
00:10:22.725 --> 00:10:29.312
And I'm pretty sure, because Mom was real good at keeping pictures . . . Getting photographs from whoever, somebody.

160
00:10:29.312 --> 00:10:30.862
And my dad too.

161
00:10:30.862 --> 00:10:32.800
Now he loved taking pictures.

162
00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:34.738
He introduced me to cameras.

163
00:10:34.738 --> 00:10:35.900
Yeah so . . . Yeah.

164
00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of camera would he use?

165
00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:41.892
<v EDWARDS>I started off with a Polaroid.

166
00:10:41.892 --> 00:10:45.265
I don't even know if you remember

167
00:10:45.265 --> 00:10:45.747
<c.p>them.

168
00:10:45.747 --> 00:10:46.711
Oh okay!

169
00:10:46.711 --> 00:10:47.193
Yeah.

170
00:10:47.193 --> 00:10:50.566
<v ARSENEAULT>It's hard to find them . . . Those now.

171
00:10:50.566 --> 00:10:53.940
<v EDWARDS>Oh yeah, it's just a real snapshot.

172
00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:58.759
Wait tear it off . . . Shake and wait for it, yeah.

173
00:10:58.759 --> 00:11:02.614
Now my dad he always had . . . Oh, shoot.

174
00:11:02.614 --> 00:11:06.470
It was just a regular cameras like that.

175
00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:10.325
Like a thirty-five millimeter or something like that.

176
00:11:10.325 --> 00:11:15.627
Yeah that what he would . . . he was . . . Like I say better.

177
00:11:15.627 --> 00:11:17.554
He was into it.

178
00:11:17.554 --> 00:11:24.301
That was my dad whenever he got into something he got into it heavy.

179
00:11:24.301 --> 00:11:30.566
He did it just like his reading; he got his own library . . . cameras.

180
00:11:30.566 --> 00:11:36.349
I mean he just went all out of the way with them.

181
00:11:36.349 --> 00:11:47.434
His dress . . . I'm thinking like maybe twice, three times a year he would buy a suit and a pair of shoes to match.

182
00:11:47.434 --> 00:11:52.735
I mean he was . . . He just got into things like that.

183
00:11:52.735 --> 00:11:55.627
And I just got into sports.

184
00:11:55.627 --> 00:11:59.000
Yes, but he was pretty cool.

185
00:11:59.000 --> 00:12:03.364
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it like being a pastor's child?

186
00:12:03.364 --> 00:12:11.545
<v EDWARDS>To me it was fun . . . Entertaining because I would follow him everywhere he would go.

187
00:12:11.545 --> 00:12:20.273
Like on Sundays whenever he would go preach I would follow him as far as Vinton,

188
00:12:20.273 --> 00:12:27.364
Jennings, [cities in Louisiana] and surrounding areas Lake Charles [Louisiana] and all that.

189
00:12:27.364 --> 00:12:32.273
He pretty much was kind of like my idol.

190
00:12:32.273 --> 00:12:40.455
We got along real good and got a chance to meet a lot of folks.

191
00:12:40.455 --> 00:12:53.000
I guess that's why my personality is like it is because he exposed me to people and I really got in to that.

192
00:12:53.000 --> 00:12:56.273
Even today I'm the same way.

193
00:12:56.273 --> 00:13:00.636
That’s about all I can say about that.

194
00:13:00.636 --> 00:13:02.818
But it was fun.

195
00:13:02.818 --> 00:13:09.909
Real experiencing . . . I had a good time shall I say growing with him.

196
00:13:09.909 --> 00:13:20.273
And of course between each ride there was always a conversation he kept me with my mind open towards

197
00:13:20.273 --> 00:13:20.818
<c.p>things.

198
00:13:20.818 --> 00:13:30.091
That's why I don't jump to harsh decisions . . . I don’t make quick decisions or anything like that.

199
00:13:30.091 --> 00:13:32.273
Pretty much very patient.

200
00:13:32.273 --> 00:13:35.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did he preach?

201
00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:39.613
<v EDWARDS>I know it was Saint James Baptist Church.

202
00:13:39.613 --> 00:13:41.919
That was in Vinton.

203
00:13:41.919 --> 00:13:48.838
I don't know the . . . Remember the name of the church in Jennings.

204
00:13:48.838 --> 00:13:52.297
Evergreen Baptist Church in Lake Charles.

205
00:13:52.297 --> 00:13:59.793
But I think the only church that he pastored at was in Vinton.

206
00:13:59.793 --> 00:14:10.171
And he was like, I guess you could say like a fill-in pastor at Evergreen in Lake Charles.

207
00:14:10.171 --> 00:14:19.973
He wasn't the appointed pastor or anything like that; he wasn’t voted in or anything like that.

208
00:14:19.973 --> 00:14:30.928
He just . . . When the regular pastor couldn't be there or was sick or whatever, he would go and fill-in.

209
00:14:30.928 --> 00:14:37.270
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he ever tell y'all about how he got his calling?

210
00:14:37.270 --> 00:14:39.000
Or when?

211
00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:45.006
<v EDWARDS>He may have, to be honest with you, but I couldn't tell you.

212
00:14:45.006 --> 00:14:51.937
I don't think it was something that was interesting to me at that particular time.

213
00:14:51.937 --> 00:15:05.335
Because we . . . Before he became a minister every Sunday morning we would have a prayer at the house before we even went to Sunday school or went to church.

214
00:15:05.335 --> 00:15:09.032
So that was just a routine for us.

215
00:15:09.032 --> 00:15:17.348
Come to think of it . . . Now that I'm thinking about it I always thought he was a minister.

216
00:15:17.348 --> 00:15:23.816
But I know he got his calling like . . . I think it was like ’62.

217
00:15:23.816 --> 00:15:27.051
1962 or ’63 or something like that.

218
00:15:27.051 --> 00:15:33.981
Yeah but shoot think about it right now . . . I'm thinking he always was a minister.

219
00:15:33.981 --> 00:15:37.677
Yeah but that's all I knew him from.

220
00:15:37.677 --> 00:15:49.690
Like I say religion’s been in our household even when we were small we would have prayer every Sunday morning before we went to Sunday school.

221
00:15:49.690 --> 00:15:52.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What is Sunday school?

222
00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:56.681
<v EDWARDS>That's where you go and . . . It's like a Bible study.

223
00:15:56.681 --> 00:15:59.957
You go in and have a devotion.

224
00:15:59.957 --> 00:16:06.511
You have Sunday school class where you have teachers; they teach us the Bible.

225
00:16:06.511 --> 00:16:14.000
Then after the lessons we give high points as to what we learned for that day.

226
00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:22.894
And that's right before the regular 11 AM services started and that was every Sunday we’d go through that.

227
00:16:22.894 --> 00:16:23.362
Yeah.

228
00:16:23.362 --> 00:16:29.447
<v ARSENEAULT>Were you expected to behave any differently because you were . . . Your dad was . . .?

229
00:16:29.447 --> 00:16:31.319
<v EDWARDS>Oh of course [laughs].

230
00:16:31.319 --> 00:16:36.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How were you expected to behave around adults and . . .?

231
00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:43.845
<v EDWARDS>You're supposed to be . . . You not supposed to even be heard of unless spoken to.

232
00:16:43.845 --> 00:16:47.506
So yeah we were well mannered; behaved.

233
00:16:47.506 --> 00:16:51.167
We didn't give no trouble, no nothing.

234
00:16:51.167 --> 00:17:00.580
We just did what we were supposed to do and that was it and that was the norm.

235
00:17:00.580 --> 00:17:09.994
And otherwise we would really get it if not mainly from him, but Mom would see to it.

236
00:17:09.994 --> 00:17:10.517
Yeah.

237
00:17:10.517 --> 00:17:16.270
<v ARSENEAULT>Who did . . . Who took care of the discipline in the family?

238
00:17:16.270 --> 00:17:17.839
<v EDWARDS>They both did.

239
00:17:17.839 --> 00:17:23.069
Once my mom would chastise us whatever, she leaves it

240
00:17:23.069 --> 00:17:27.776
<c.p>open for Dad when he got home from work.

241
00:17:27.776 --> 00:17:39.282
He heard about it, and if he felt that disciplinary action should be taken on his part also, he would do that.

242
00:17:39.282 --> 00:17:42.943
But yeah they both shared with it.

243
00:17:42.943 --> 00:17:48.172
Yeah, I can tell you plenty of stories about them.

244
00:17:48.172 --> 00:17:55.494
Don’t get me wrong, I was a mischievous child; yes, I got my share.

245
00:17:55.494 --> 00:17:56.540
Yes indeed.

246
00:17:56.540 --> 00:18:00.724
I guess today you would call it abuse.

247
00:18:00.724 --> 00:18:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things would you do to get in trouble?

248
00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:09.392
<v EDWARDS>My big thing was throwing.

249
00:18:09.392 --> 00:18:14.177
I’d pick up rocks and throw . . . Bust windows on cars.

250
00:18:14.177 --> 00:18:21.833
Me and my brother Kenney we would war especially when they come and grade the roads.

251
00:18:21.833 --> 00:18:32.839
We used to have these shells and when you throw them they go off straight they'll rise and curve or something like that.

252
00:18:32.839 --> 00:18:37.145
So we would take and war against each other.

253
00:18:37.145 --> 00:18:44.801
We’d be like maybe fifty yards from each other in ditches or whatever and behind trees.

254
00:18:44.801 --> 00:18:56.763
But we take our collection and throw it and we’d try to time it to where we’d know he going to peep out and throw,

255
00:18:56.763 --> 00:19:02.505
so we would throw those shells like I was telling you about.

256
00:19:02.505 --> 00:19:10.161
And you can tell which one is going to go low and come up and curve.

257
00:19:10.161 --> 00:19:12.075
So we time it.

258
00:19:12.075 --> 00:19:12.554
Yes.

259
00:19:12.554 --> 00:19:13.511
Very mischievous.

260
00:19:13.511 --> 00:19:17.339
We used to have a lot of fun.

261
00:19:17.339 --> 00:19:23.559
And when they find out about that yes they would, "Come on in."

262
00:19:23.559 --> 00:19:29.301
And you know you're in trouble when they call you, "Barry Wayne.

263
00:19:29.301 --> 00:19:33.129
Come on in here it's your turn!” [laughs].

264
00:19:33.129 --> 00:19:36.000
<v ARSENAULT>They just lined y'all up?

265
00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:36.453
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

266
00:19:36.453 --> 00:19:36.906
Yes.

267
00:19:36.906 --> 00:19:43.249
But it was . . . Like I said, to us it was sort of like fun.

268
00:19:43.249 --> 00:19:53.669
Even catching a spanking you knew you did something wrong and you sorry for it but that was just part of growing up.

269
00:19:53.669 --> 00:20:00.012
You kind of like look forward to a spanking because I guess we don't

270
00:20:00.012 --> 00:20:09.980
<c.p>know that we could never do everything right or all things right so we expect you know what we weren't sure of,

271
00:20:09.980 --> 00:20:16.322
“Well yeah we probably get a spanking for this but we try it anyway.”

272
00:20:16.322 --> 00:20:16.776
Yes.

273
00:20:16.776 --> 00:20:21.306
<v ARSENEAULT>What other kinds of things would y'all do for fun?

274
00:20:21.306 --> 00:20:24.024
<v EDWARDS>Well we had a little games.

275
00:20:24.024 --> 00:20:28.102
This was before I got into girls or whatever.

276
00:20:28.102 --> 00:20:32.180
We used to like marbles; used to play marbles.

277
00:20:32.180 --> 00:20:33.992
We had spinning tops.

278
00:20:33.992 --> 00:20:38.069
I don't know if y'all are familiar with them?

279
00:20:38.069 --> 00:20:38.522
Yeah.

280
00:20:38.522 --> 00:20:39.429
Yeah, yeah.

281
00:20:39.429 --> 00:20:46.678
You take them tie them up on a string and yeah . . . We used to play that.

282
00:20:46.678 --> 00:20:55.739
We would get together as neighborhoods like where you are right now, this would be the east side of Mossville.

283
00:20:55.739 --> 00:21:03.441
Okay, on the other side of Prater Road where I grew up, that was the west side.

284
00:21:03.441 --> 00:21:10.690
And we would meet up on the weekends . . . Yeah right behind here where the park is.

285
00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:16.580
Before they had baseball we used to play football in that little park.

286
00:21:16.580 --> 00:21:22.922
And we used to meet there . . . Call it sandlot football and just play football.

287
00:21:22.922 --> 00:21:25.188
The east against the west.

288
00:21:25.188 --> 00:21:27.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who would win?

289
00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:32.942
<v EDWARDS>I'm going to say this side we usually win, because they had the bigger guys.

290
00:21:32.942 --> 00:21:41.656
Like, matter of fact right across this street almost here had the family called the [Dellafosses?] and they were some big guys.

291
00:21:41.656 --> 00:21:42.844
All of them.

292
00:21:42.844 --> 00:21:48.390
Even the baby . . . The one younger than me was about ten [feet] two [inches].

293
00:21:48.390 --> 00:21:55.519
Weighed about five-hundred pounds. [laughs] So every time they would come out you'd say, "Oh Lord" you know.

294
00:21:55.519 --> 00:21:59.481
But we never stopped playing; we played our hearts out.

295
00:21:59.481 --> 00:22:03.838
But they would pretty much win the majority of the times.

296
00:22:03.838 --> 00:22:10.175
Now unless the Dellafosse boys went on a trip or something, then they couldn't make it,

297
00:22:10.175 --> 00:22:12.948
<c.p>then we’d have a lot of fun.

298
00:22:12.948 --> 00:22:18.097
Matter of fact one of the other guys I was talking to Payne.

299
00:22:18.097 --> 00:22:21.266
Yeah Blackie . . . That's what we called him, Eddie.

300
00:22:21.266 --> 00:22:26.019
I don't know what y'all call him, but he was here earlier.

301
00:22:26.019 --> 00:22:28.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh Mr. Joseph Payne?

302
00:22:28.000 --> 00:22:28.440
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

303
00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:28.881
Joseph.

304
00:22:28.881 --> 00:22:29.321
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

305
00:22:29.321 --> 00:22:34.606
<v EDWARDS>And he and his brothers . . . He has quite a few brothers too.

306
00:22:34.606 --> 00:22:37.249
Yeah, and there was another group.

307
00:22:37.249 --> 00:22:41.212
See all of them was on the same teams.

308
00:22:41.212 --> 00:22:49.580
See we didn't have that many but we had a lot of people, but we didn't have nobody big.

309
00:22:49.580 --> 00:22:54.425
All ours was mixed around speed and we did pretty good.

310
00:22:54.425 --> 00:23:03.233
We kind of like have our own . . . But that was some of the things we did to pass our times.

311
00:23:03.233 --> 00:23:08.518
We did a lot of swimming at the swimming pool right here.

312
00:23:08.518 --> 00:23:12.041
I think that was the thing to do.

313
00:23:12.041 --> 00:23:16.446
Our biggest pastime would be swimming, because everybody got involved.

314
00:23:16.446 --> 00:23:21.290
The lifeguards and the director over the swimming pool and all.

315
00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:28.337
They used to set it up to where we'd have swim meets and stuff like this.

316
00:23:28.337 --> 00:23:30.979
At that time lifeguards taught swimming.

317
00:23:30.979 --> 00:23:37.585
You didn't have to go get certified at the Red Cross or anything like that.

318
00:23:37.585 --> 00:23:50.358
Well the lifeguards had to get certified but they didn't have to get certified to teach swimming so we learned . . . The basic crawl swim stroke was the American crawl.

319
00:23:50.358 --> 00:23:53.000
So everybody learned that and . . .

320
00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:55.388
<v ARENSEAULT>Is that like a breaststroke?

321
00:23:55.388 --> 00:23:58.253
<v EDWARDS>It was just stroke over hand.

322
00:23:58.253 --> 00:23:58.731
Yeah.

323
00:23:58.731 --> 00:23:59.208
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

324
00:23:59.208 --> 00:23:59.686
Yeah.

325
00:23:59.686 --> 00:24:09.714
<v EDWARDS>And after we learned that we picked up . . . I guess you could say on our own breaststroke, backstroke, stuff like that.

326
00:24:09.714 --> 00:24:16.400
But at the age of six years old you could figure I was swimming.

327
00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:23.563
And say I was just starting school and we were swimming . . . Diving off the boards.

328
00:24:23.563 --> 00:24:35.024
And that's why I say on my Braxton’s when I was early Darnell he and I were like I said around the same age,

329
00:24:35.024 --> 00:24:44.576
but we were one of the first two at the age of fifteen that became lifeguards at the pool here.

330
00:24:44.576 --> 00:24:49.351
And he could dive so he would teach me diving.

331
00:24:49.351 --> 00:24:52.216
And of course he could swim.

332
00:24:52.216 --> 00:24:54.604
I mean fast competition style.

333
00:24:54.604 --> 00:25:05.588
So and then he knew that I could swim so we would go on these swim meets together in DeRidder, DeQuincy [Louisiana cities].

334
00:25:05.588 --> 00:25:10.363
And I guess you can say that's pretty much it.

335
00:25:10.363 --> 00:25:14.184
But we had . . . Riding bicycles was another one.

336
00:25:14.184 --> 00:25:16.094
Swimming and riding bicycles.

337
00:25:16.094 --> 00:25:21.824
The sports that we had like I said, it was mainly football.

338
00:25:21.824 --> 00:25:29.465
And after school they had recreation league were we played you baseball; had uniforms and everything.

339
00:25:29.465 --> 00:25:38.539
And by that time fifteen, sixteen years old would take a little nip every once and a while [laughs].

340
00:25:38.539 --> 00:25:46.180
So that would be the fun . . . You get in there and try to hit the ball.

341
00:25:46.180 --> 00:25:50.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Can't even make it to the plate.

342
00:25:50.000 --> 00:25:51.667
<v EDWARDS>Now we didn't get wasted.

343
00:25:51.667 --> 00:25:53.000
Just having a little . . .

344
00:25:53.000 --> 00:25:53.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

345
00:25:53.333 --> 00:25:54.333
What was it?

346
00:25:54.333 --> 00:25:56.000
What were y'all drinking?

347
00:25:56.000 --> 00:25:56.609
<v EDWARDS>Wine.

348
00:25:56.609 --> 00:25:59.652
Morgan . . . No, not Morgan David.

349
00:25:59.652 --> 00:26:00.870
MD 20/20.

350
00:26:00.870 --> 00:26:01.478
Yeah.

351
00:26:01.478 --> 00:26:03.913
<v ARSENEAULT>Never heard of it.

352
00:26:03.913 --> 00:26:09.391
<v EDWARDS>I don't even think they make it any more.

353
00:26:09.391 --> 00:26:17.913
And then we used to drink Tokay, I don't think they make that anymore.

354
00:26:17.913 --> 00:26:18.522
Swiss-up.

355
00:26:18.522 --> 00:26:21.565
Yeah that's pretty much . . . Yeah.

356
00:26:21.565 --> 00:26:30.087
But we would have us a little taste especially before going to play baseball.

357
00:26:30.087 --> 00:26:30.696
Yeah.

358
00:26:30.696 --> 00:26:31.913
And we . . .

359
00:26:31.913 --> 00:26:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did they play football here at the Rigmaiden Center?

360
00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:41.505
<v EDWARDS>Once they closed the school, yes.

361
00:26:41.505 --> 00:26:46.178
Joseph Payne, his brother, we call him Sunny.

362
00:26:46.178 --> 00:26:49.099
He had a team here.

363
00:26:49.099 --> 00:26:53.188
It was like a little league team.

364
00:26:53.188 --> 00:26:57.861
They played right out here in the back.

365
00:26:57.861 --> 00:27:10.129
But by that time I had . . . I was either in Houston [Texas] for work . . . Yeah I'm thinking I was in Houston.

366
00:27:10.129 --> 00:27:12.465
Houston or Port Arthur.

367
00:27:12.465 --> 00:27:21.812
But I never got involved with extracurricular like that for you know with kids and all.

368
00:27:21.812 --> 00:27:29.990
<v ARSENEAULT>I wanted to ask you about . . . You said you served in the air force?

369
00:27:29.990 --> 00:27:30.574
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

370
00:27:30.574 --> 00:27:31.158
Yes.

371
00:27:31.158 --> 00:27:37.000
<v ARSENAULT>I wanted to ask you the year for that.

372
00:27:37.000 --> 00:27:49.000
<v EDWARDS>December the seventh 1970 through November twenty-fifth, 1974.

373
00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:50.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

374
00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:52.000
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

375
00:27:52.000 --> 00:28:04.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about boot camp?

376
00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:06.625
<v EDWARDS>It was . . . I guess you could say . . .

377
00:28:06.625 --> 00:28:07.750
<v ARSENEAULT>Where was it?

378
00:28:07.750 --> 00:28:09.250
<v EDWARDS>In San Antonio, Texas.

379
00:28:09.250 --> 00:28:10.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

380
00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:13.802
<v EDWARDS>Yeah I was in the air force.

381
00:28:13.802 --> 00:28:15.431
It was strange.

382
00:28:15.431 --> 00:28:24.664
I was . . . It was myself and two other blacks was in this squadron of about sixty people.

383
00:28:24.664 --> 00:28:32.810
That was . . . I guess you could say that was the biggest shock that I had.

384
00:28:32.810 --> 00:28:43.672
Of course my sports and what I could do, especially in basic training, I mean I could run a distance.

385
00:28:43.672 --> 00:28:48.560
I could pretty much pass any kind of physical

386
00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:51.819
<c.p>fitness programs they put you through.

387
00:28:51.819 --> 00:28:56.164
All of that was . . . It was like fun.

388
00:28:56.164 --> 00:29:01.595
I really had a real good time in basic training.

389
00:29:01.595 --> 00:29:07.569
It was like fun; It was like P.E. [Physical Education] [laughs]

390
00:29:07.569 --> 00:29:13.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of things did they have you do?

391
00:29:13.000 --> 00:29:14.830
<v EDWARDS>Pretty much the basics.

392
00:29:14.830 --> 00:29:19.404
Actually, besides physical you had to register with a weapon.

393
00:29:19.404 --> 00:29:21.233
I almost achieved expert.

394
00:29:21.233 --> 00:29:26.722
Then you had your classes depending on which field you were in.

395
00:29:26.722 --> 00:29:29.009
I was in security police.

396
00:29:29.009 --> 00:29:33.126
That's where I went through most of my training.

397
00:29:33.126 --> 00:29:39.529
Then you’re pretty much doing what a cop would do except for guarding airplanes.

398
00:29:39.529 --> 00:29:49.592
In basic training walking around with a toy gun patrolling I guess you could say I call that some kind of duty.

399
00:29:49.592 --> 00:29:50.507
Guard duty?

400
00:29:50.507 --> 00:29:50.964
Yeah.

401
00:29:50.964 --> 00:29:53.709
That's what our guard duty was.

402
00:29:53.709 --> 00:29:56.910
Looking over planes and stuff like that.

403
00:29:56.910 --> 00:30:02.399
But boot camp was . . . I guess you could say it was fun.

404
00:30:02.399 --> 00:30:06.973
I mean then you look at it as a job.

405
00:30:06.973 --> 00:30:12.919
They'll be looking at you to wonder, “Can I depend on this guy?”

406
00:30:12.919 --> 00:30:19.780
They always knew they could depend on me, “He's going be at that goddamn plane.

407
00:30:19.780 --> 00:30:28.471
He's not going to take a break and go see his girlfriend without telling somebody or anything like that.

408
00:30:28.471 --> 00:30:33.045
He's going to be doing what he's supposed to do.

409
00:30:33.045 --> 00:30:37.619
And when it's time for him to knock-off he'll knock-off.

410
00:30:37.619 --> 00:30:41.735
But if you still need him he'll be there.”

411
00:30:41.735 --> 00:30:46.309
I guess I got that from growing up around home.

412
00:30:46.309 --> 00:30:46.767
Yeah.

413
00:30:46.767 --> 00:30:49.969
But like I said it was fun.

414
00:30:49.969 --> 00:30:55.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you get stationed anywhere out of the United States?

415
00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:55.461
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

416
00:30:55.461 --> 00:31:05.132
That was real interesting since you said that . . . My first place of duty was Barksdale air force Base in Shreveport [Louisiana],

417
00:31:05.132 --> 00:31:06.974
or Bossier City [Louisiana].

418
00:31:06.974 --> 00:31:11.118
And I served there . . . That was my permanent station.

419
00:31:11.118 --> 00:31:13.421
So they usually send us

420
00:31:13.421 --> 00:31:14.803
<c.p>temporary duty status.

421
00:31:14.803 --> 00:31:21.711
I was there for about five months then they sent me TDY they called it.

422
00:31:21.711 --> 00:31:24.474
Temporary duty to the Philippine Islands.

423
00:31:24.474 --> 00:31:26.316
Stayed there three months.

424
00:31:26.316 --> 00:31:30.000
Then from there they sent me to Taiwan.

425
00:31:30.000 --> 00:31:35.526
Stayed there a few months then they sent me to Okinawa [Japan].

426
00:31:35.526 --> 00:31:39.671
Stayed there a few months then back to Shreveport.

427
00:31:39.671 --> 00:31:49.342
Stayed there about a year then they sent me permanent station to the Philippine Islands and that's where I ended up.

428
00:31:49.342 --> 00:31:56.711
Then I did about fifteen months there and by the time of my separation from there,

429
00:31:56.711 --> 00:32:01.776
it was time for me to get out of the military.

430
00:32:01.776 --> 00:32:02.697
Yeah but . . .

431
00:32:02.697 --> 00:32:05.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What base was it?

432
00:32:05.000 --> 00:32:07.235
<v EDWARDS>Clark Air Force Base.

433
00:32:07.235 --> 00:32:10.029
That was in the Philippines.

434
00:32:10.029 --> 00:32:13.382
Kadena [Air Base] was in Okinawa.

435
00:32:13.382 --> 00:32:24.559
I can't think of the one in Taiwan for nothing, but I was just there for a couple of months.

436
00:32:24.559 --> 00:32:26.794
Yeah that was it.

437
00:32:26.794 --> 00:32:30.706
<v ARSENEAULT>What was your M.O.S. [Military Occupation Specialty]?

438
00:32:30.706 --> 00:32:31.824
<v EDWARDS>Administrative specialist.

439
00:32:31.824 --> 00:32:49.706
I didn't pursue . . . I guess after I grew up a little bit I didn't think guarding airplanes was something I really wanted to do so I went in as an administrative specialist.

440
00:32:49.706 --> 00:32:52.500
What they call a seven-oh-two.

441
00:32:52.500 --> 00:32:56.412
It's just like human resources, like that.

442
00:32:56.412 --> 00:33:03.676
And then I also qualified under that same M.O.S. as a postal clerk.

443
00:33:03.676 --> 00:33:09.824
So that's where I finished up my time in the Philippines.

444
00:33:09.824 --> 00:33:14.294
I was postal clerk; just did the mail.

445
00:33:14.294 --> 00:33:21.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you get to travel at all when you were there?

446
00:33:21.000 --> 00:33:24.243
<v EDWARDS>While I was in the Philippines?

447
00:33:24.243 --> 00:33:24.784
<v ARSENEAULT>[Agrees].

448
00:33:24.784 --> 00:33:28.027
<v EDWARDS>I went to Subic Bay once.

449
00:33:28.027 --> 00:33:31.270
And I went to Manilla once.

450
00:33:31.270 --> 00:33:39.919
And bachelor on leave . . . We had fun and that's all I want to say about that.

451
00:33:39.919 --> 00:33:44.784
That’s all I need to say about that. [laughs]

452
00:33:44.784 --> 00:33:45.865
<v ARSENEAULT>Fair enough.

453
00:33:45.865 --> 00:33:48.568
My brother's in the army.

454
00:33:48.568 --> 00:33:49.649
I understand.

455
00:33:49.649 --> 00:33:50.730
<v EDWARDS>Right yeah.

456
00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:51.811
That's right.

457
00:33:51.811 --> 00:33:56.135
He'll be able to tell you if he . . .

458
00:33:56.135 --> 00:33:56.676
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

459
00:33:56.676 --> 00:33:59.919
<v EDWARDS>If he felt the need to.

460
00:33:59.919 --> 00:34:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Right.

461
00:34:01.000 --> 00:34:01.364
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

462
00:34:01.364 --> 00:34:05.364
<v ARSENEAULT>So any vivid memories of your time in the air force?

463
00:34:05.364 --> 00:34:06.455
Anything stand out?

464
00:34:06.455 --> 00:34:06.818
<v EDWARDS>No.

465
00:34:06.818 --> 00:34:09.000
<v ARSENEAULT>That you want to share.

466
00:34:09.000 --> 00:34:12.969
<v EDWARDS>No. I know that for a fact.

467
00:34:12.969 --> 00:34:19.773
Oh I guess you could say that's where I first learned prejudice.

468
00:34:19.773 --> 00:34:27.711
I was in Biloxi . . . I was in Bossier City at Barksdale air force Base.

469
00:34:27.711 --> 00:34:41.887
And the field that I was in, in my class, I think they had maybe about two or three blacks, and the rest were white.

470
00:34:41.887 --> 00:34:46.423
And so I . . . My friends became white folks.

471
00:34:46.423 --> 00:35:00.598
And we were downtown Shreveport going to clubs and went to this first club we walked in the bar they met us at the door,

472
00:35:00.598 --> 00:35:06.835
“You guys can come in but you, you can't go in.”

473
00:35:06.835 --> 00:35:10.237
They wanted to start a fight.

474
00:35:10.237 --> 00:35:12.505
I said, "No man.

475
00:35:12.505 --> 00:35:15.340
Let's just go somewhere else."

476
00:35:15.340 --> 00:35:20.443
Went there . . . Somewhere else and the same thing happened.

477
00:35:20.443 --> 00:35:31.784
So by that time I was frustrated and, "Man, just take me back to the base and y'all go party."

478
00:35:31.784 --> 00:35:33.485
And did that.

479
00:35:33.485 --> 00:35:40.856
So while I was in my room . . . Edwin Edwards, he was congressman then,

480
00:35:40.856 --> 00:35:48.227
and I sit and I wrote him a letter explaining what had happened.

481
00:35:48.227 --> 00:35:52.763
Shipped it off to him and he responded.

482
00:35:52.763 --> 00:35:59.000
And he sent guys from the Pentagon to the base.

483
00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:10.535
And when they came in the building you could hear down the hall, I was in the supply section, you can hear down the hall, "Squadron,

484
00:36:10.535 --> 00:36:23.400
attention!" indoors and everything and I . . . They hadn't gotten to my place just yet so I’m fiddling around trying to get set up and you can hear them saying,

485
00:36:23.400 --> 00:36:25.618
"We're looking for [Airman?] Edwards."

486
00:36:25.618 --> 00:36:27.393
I said, "Oh shoot."

487
00:36:27.393 --> 00:36:29.611
So they're looking for me.

488
00:36:29.611 --> 00:36:38.040
So I hurried and got around to my desk so when they came into my section . . . Call us attention.

489
00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:44.251
Guy came right over started talking to me and he said, "You Airman Edwards?"

490
00:36:44.251 --> 00:36:48.244
He said, "Anything . . . We need you to do something."

491
00:36:48.244 --> 00:36:52.680
They had me to go and talk to my commander.

492
00:36:52.680 --> 00:36:59.335
Tell him the story and to see what he did, what he's going to do.

493
00:36:59.335 --> 00:37:05.102
So he said he'll check back with me in a couple of weeks.

494
00:37:05.102 --> 00:37:07.320
No man . . . His own word.

495
00:37:07.320 --> 00:37:14.418
Guy from Pentagon came back a couple of weeks say anything you want to say different

496
00:37:14.418 --> 00:37:22.847
<c.p>than the statement that I gave as to how I was treated and what my commander did for me.

497
00:37:22.847 --> 00:37:27.727
I said, "No everything happened just like it was supposed to.

498
00:37:27.727 --> 00:37:32.607
My commander he told me like I shouldn't have been there.

499
00:37:32.607 --> 00:37:35.713
Talk to me like father to son.

500
00:37:35.713 --> 00:37:41.924
"Ah son, you shouldn't have been going to places like this,” stuff like this.

501
00:37:41.924 --> 00:37:47.247
And next thing we knew they sent him out to another base.

502
00:37:47.247 --> 00:37:53.458
The places that we visited downtown they marked them off limits to military personnel.

503
00:37:53.458 --> 00:38:01.000
And then when I got back on the base . . . And I felt so bad for that.

504
00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:07.979
On my duty section and in my squadron they used to ask me to do chores.

505
00:38:07.979 --> 00:38:19.757
And I felt so bad on that because you’re given orders . . . I'm in the military you treat me just like you treat . . . You don't come ask me,

506
00:38:19.757 --> 00:38:23.247
"You think you can cut the yard today?

507
00:38:23.247 --> 00:38:25.864
You think you can wash dishes?

508
00:38:25.864 --> 00:38:30.226
You think you can go on K.P. duty or whatever?"

509
00:38:30.226 --> 00:38:35.461
They would ask me before they would . . . And I just felt bad.

510
00:38:35.461 --> 00:38:46.802
I started drinking and puffing, stuff like that and I guess you could say that's where I really got introduced to doing some type of drugs.

511
00:38:46.802 --> 00:38:48.111
I felt alienated.

512
00:38:48.111 --> 00:38:49.420
I felt alone.

513
00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:52.473
That's the only thing that stands out.

514
00:38:52.473 --> 00:39:02.506
And I couldn't wait for my three years to finish up so I could get out and start my life all over again.

515
00:39:02.506 --> 00:39:04.687
Which I had to do.

516
00:39:04.687 --> 00:39:08.613
<v ARSENEAULT>You just wanted to be treated like everybody else?

517
00:39:08.613 --> 00:39:09.049
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

518
00:39:09.049 --> 00:39:11.667
I don't want no special treatment.

519
00:39:11.667 --> 00:39:12.975
No, not even.

520
00:39:12.975 --> 00:39:24.753
I can see them putting the places that we went you know off limits to military personnel because it could have been a different type of incident.

521
00:39:24.753 --> 00:39:29.115
We could have went in there and fight broke out.

522
00:39:29.115 --> 00:39:31.296
Somebody get killed or whatever.

523
00:39:31.296 --> 00:39:35.658
I can see them putting it off limits like that.

524
00:39:35.658 --> 00:39:42.202
But to come and ask me to do something that I'm supposed to be doing?

525
00:39:42.202 --> 00:39:46.128
No. So that favoritism stuff, I don't like that.

526
00:39:46.128 --> 00:39:47.000
No.

527
00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:49.850
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about integration?

528
00:39:49.850 --> 00:39:51.275
<v EDWARDS>What about it?

529
00:39:51.275 --> 00:39:56.025
<v ARSENEAULT>Where you still in school when that happened in Mossville?

530
00:39:56.025 --> 00:39:59.350
<v EDWARDS>No. My class was the last class . . .

531
00:39:59.350 --> 00:40:00.300
<v ARSENEAULT>That's right.

532
00:40:00.300 --> 00:40:03.150
That's right you told me that.

533
00:40:03.150 --> 00:40:03.625
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

534
00:40:03.625 --> 00:40:06.000
Yes, the last class.

535
00:40:06.000 --> 00:40:08.979
<v ARSENEAULT>So you never got to experience that until you left?

536
00:40:08.979 --> 00:40:10.170
<v EDWARDS>Went to the military.

537
00:40:10.170 --> 00:40:10.766
<v ARSENEAULT>I understand.

538
00:40:10.766 --> 00:40:11.064
Okay.

539
00:40:11.064 --> 00:40:11.362
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

540
00:40:11.362 --> 00:40:17.617
Matter of fact I went . . . Before I went in the military I did a year at Southern University in Baton Rouge.

541
00:40:17.617 --> 00:40:19.404
And it's just around all blacks.

542
00:40:19.404 --> 00:40:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

543
00:40:20.000 --> 00:40:20.632
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

544
00:40:20.632 --> 00:40:21.263
Yes.

545
00:40:21.263 --> 00:40:21.895
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

546
00:40:21.895 --> 00:40:34.526
<v EDWARDS>Yes, and like I said that was something new to me very much, or I just didn’t never noticed it.

547
00:40:34.526 --> 00:40:44.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Did anything else kind of shock you about being around so many white people?

548
00:40:44.000 --> 00:40:48.467
<v EDWARDS>There was . . . I never really said it,

549
00:40:48.467 --> 00:41:00.590
but I guess coming back talking before they . . . People just like me. [laughs] They just like me, you know?

550
00:41:00.590 --> 00:41:10.800
Like I said, when I was in the military majority of my friends were white folks.

551
00:41:10.800 --> 00:41:19.095
I guess because of the . . . My branch of the military I was in,

552
00:41:19.095 --> 00:41:29.305
for one; the section of my duty AFC that's not appealing to you know black folks.

553
00:41:29.305 --> 00:41:43.981
Which my . . . I guess I scored high enough to be put in something else than regular where they would put blacks or whatever.

554
00:41:43.981 --> 00:41:54.190
But a lot of folks . . . Even my roommate was white and shoot we had a ball.

555
00:41:54.190 --> 00:41:59.295
Tell you y'all can drink more than me.

556
00:41:59.295 --> 00:42:06.952
I can do a little drinking but those jokers drink a lot.

557
00:42:06.952 --> 00:42:17.800
And one of them was country western so, “Yee-haw!” [laughs] That was one his main little phrases.

558
00:42:17.800 --> 00:42:22.905
Yeah but it was very, very, very interesting.

559
00:42:22.905 --> 00:42:32.476
Matter of fact when I got out of the military and got different jobs around,

560
00:42:32.476 --> 00:42:40.133
which was no problem, I always had to decide to re-enlist again.

561
00:42:40.133 --> 00:42:56.724
But . . . And after about not a good six months out I landed a job with a company that I retired from just about six years ago.

562
00:42:56.724 --> 00:42:58.000
And . . .

563
00:42:58.000 --> 00:42:58.938
<v ARSENEAULT>Firestone [Tire and Rubber Company]?

564
00:42:58.938 --> 00:42:59.138
<v EDWARDS>Yeah.

565
00:42:59.125 --> 00:42:59.875
How did you know?

566
00:42:59.875 --> 00:43:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You mentioned it last time.

567
00:43:01.000 --> 00:43:01.476
<v EDWARDS>Okay.

568
00:43:01.476 --> 00:43:02.429
She's sharp.

569
00:43:02.429 --> 00:43:02.905
Yes.

570
00:43:02.905 --> 00:43:04.810
<v ARSENEAULT>It's all coming back.

571
00:43:04.810 --> 00:43:05.286
<v EDWARDS>Okay.

572
00:43:05.286 --> 00:43:05.762
Yeah.

573
00:43:05.762 --> 00:43:09.095
So it was a lot of fun.

574
00:43:09.095 --> 00:43:14.333
Like I said my sixty-four years here have been most rewarding.

575
00:43:14.333 --> 00:43:15.286
No complaints.

576
00:43:15.286 --> 00:43:16.238
No nothing.

577
00:43:16.238 --> 00:43:29.095
And y'all giving me the chance to look back and bring out things that I never really would have thought about and see now that I'm thankful.

578
00:43:29.095 --> 00:43:33.857
I'm very thankful for the way I was brought up.

579
00:43:33.857 --> 00:43:38.143
For . . . Like I said these folks right in here.

580
00:43:38.143 --> 00:43:39.571
Kim's mom and . . .

581
00:43:39.571 --> 00:43:41.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Ms. Julia?

582
00:43:41.000 --> 00:43:42.714
<v EDWARDS>Yeah and her brother.

583
00:43:42.714 --> 00:43:44.857
I just . . . And we’re related.

584
00:43:44.857 --> 00:43:51.714
And today was the first day that I put it together, “Hey they brother and sister.”

585
00:43:51.714 --> 00:43:56.000
So I mean it's been a learning thing for me.

586
00:43:56.000 --> 00:44:01.571
You just don’t know . . . I be glad when y'all say y'all coming by.

587
00:44:01.571 --> 00:44:03.286
Besides the good eating . . .

588
00:44:03.286 --> 00:44:04.143
<v ARSENEAULT>Of course.

589
00:44:04.143 --> 00:44:05.000
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

590
00:44:05.000 --> 00:44:06.753
<v ARSENEAULT>Well we're at forty-five minutes.

591
00:44:06.753 --> 00:44:10.610
Is it okay if we stop now and I'll come back?

592
00:44:10.610 --> 00:44:10.961
<v EDWARDS>Oh . . .

593
00:44:10.961 --> 00:44:15.870
<v ARSENEAULT>Unless you want to keep going I can keep asking questions if you want.

594
00:44:15.870 --> 00:44:17.273
It's up to you?

595
00:44:17.273 --> 00:44:18.325
<v EDWARDS>Let's call it.

596
00:44:18.325 --> 00:44:20.429
Save it for the next time.

597
00:44:20.429 --> 00:44:20.779
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

598
00:44:20.779 --> 00:44:23.935
Alright, because you see all the questions I have.

599
00:44:23.935 --> 00:44:26.740
I have a bunch to ask you so . . .

600
00:44:26.740 --> 00:44:27.442
<v EDWARDS>Well here?

601
00:44:27.442 --> 00:44:28.143
In here?

602
00:44:28.143 --> 00:44:28.494
Yeah.

603
00:44:28.494 --> 00:44:28.844
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

604
00:44:28.844 --> 00:44:31.299
<v EDWARDS>Yeah we'll do it for next time.

605
00:44:31.299 --> 00:44:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

606
00:44:32.000 --> 00:44:36.480
<v EDWARDS>And trust me good Lord says the same thing, “I will be back again.”

607
00:44:36.480 --> 00:44:37.440
I enjoy this.

608
00:44:37.440 --> 00:44:40.640
And I was just joking about the . . . Just the eating.

609
00:44:40.640 --> 00:44:40.960
No . . .

610
00:44:40.960 --> 00:44:41.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Of course.

611
00:44:41.600 --> 00:44:41.920
Yeah.

612
00:44:41.920 --> 00:44:43.200
<v EDWARDS>I enjoy you guys.

613
00:44:43.200 --> 00:44:44.480
<v ARSENEAULT>That's just a bonus.

614
00:44:44.480 --> 00:44:44.800
<v EDWARDS>Yes.

615
00:44:44.800 --> 00:44:45.440
You’re right.

616
00:44:45.440 --> 00:44:46.080
You’re right.

617
00:44:46.080 --> 00:44:47.040
I like that.

618
00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:54.080
<v ARSENEAULT>Well thank you so much for talking with us today and I'll be in touch with you for your follow up interview.

619
00:44:54.080 --> 00:44:55.040
<v EDWARDS>You got it.

620
00:44:55.040 --> 00:44:56.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Sounds good.

621
00:44:56.000 --> 00:44:56.200
